<article class="env-proposition" id="03SX">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/03SX">Proposition <span data-tag="03SX">4.9.5.17</span>.</a> Let $F_0: \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_0 \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ and $F_1: \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_1 \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be functors of $\infty $-categories and let $\kappa $ be an uncountable cardinal. If $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_0$, $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_1$, and $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ are essentially $\kappa $-small, then the oriented fiber product $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_0 \vec{\times }_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}} \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_1$ is also essentially $\kappa $-small. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof.</strong>
      Choose equivalences of $\infty $-categories 
    <p>
<div class="equation">
  \[ \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}_0 \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_0 \quad \quad \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}\rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}\quad \quad \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}_1 \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_1, \]
</div>
<p>
       where $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}_0$, $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}_1$, and $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ are $\kappa $-small. By virtue of Remark <a data-tag="03PN" href="/tag/03PN">4.6.4.4</a>, the induced maps 
    <p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_0 \vec{\times }_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}} \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_1 \leftarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}_0 \vec{\times }_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}} \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}_1 \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}_0 \vec{\times }_{\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}} \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}_1 \]
</div>
<p>
       are equivalences of $\infty $-categories. It will therefore suffice to show that the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}_0 \vec{\times }_{\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}} \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}_1$ is $\kappa $-small. This follows from Corollaries <a data-tag="03SD" href="/tag/03SD">4.9.4.14</a> and <a data-tag="03SC" href="/tag/03SC">4.9.4.12</a>, since $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}_0 \vec{\times }_{\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}} \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}_1$ can be identified with a simplicial subset of the product $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}_0 \times \operatorname{Fun}( \Delta ^1, \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}) \times \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}_1$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
